LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — Stock as capital: the vital signs", "13", "<p>Six modules built and defended the stock truth; this module READS it — because stock is not goods on shelves, it is <b>capital wearing inventory's clothes</b>, usually the largest single asset a trading firm owns, and the reading discipline is what makes that capital WORK instead of sit.</p><p><b>The capital framing, taught to owners.</b> The ₦48m at Central is ₦48m the firm chose to hold as stock instead of cash, expansion, or debt reduction — and every stock decision is silently a capital-allocation decision: the overstock is capital parked and depreciating (expiry, obsolescence, storage); the stockout is capital ABSENT from where it earns; the dead pallet is capital in prison. The consultant who opens the analytics conversation with this framing gets the owner's attention immediately, because it converts warehouse reports into money reports — which is what they always were.</p><p><b>Health, defined.</b> Healthy stock is four things at once: the RIGHT AMOUNT (enough to serve, not enough to rot — turns and coverage, Chapter 2), in the RIGHT PLACES (the network's placement question — availability, Chapter 5), MOVING (aging and dead stock, Chapter 3), and TRUE (accuracy and valuation — modules 4-5's certificates, read here as vitals). A firm can fail any one while passing the others — the accurate, well-placed warehouse full of stock that stopped selling; the fast-turning network that stocks out weekly — which is why the health read is a PANEL of vitals, never one number, and Chapter 7 assembles the panel onto its page.</p><p><b>The reads exist because the disciplines exist.</b> Notice what this module does NOT have to build: every number it reads is exhaust from machinery already running — turns from the ledger's movements, aging from its dates, accuracy from module 4's programme, shrinkage from its reason codes, expiry losses from module 3's horizon, availability from the bins. A firm that kept modules 1-6 gets this module's analytics essentially FREE; a firm that did not has reports that read fiction — the garbage-in law that makes analytics the reward for discipline rather than its substitute. When a client asks for <i>better stock reports</i>, the diagnostic question is always whether the reports are weak or the ledger beneath them is — and by this point in the certification you know exactly how to tell.</p><p><b>The cadence law, restated for the reading room.</b> Every report in this module earns a rhythm and an owner or it is decoration (the conviction from three tracks, final appearance): the daily reads are operational (bins, exceptions), the weekly are managerial (the network dashboard's vitals), the monthly is the health page with the MD, the quarterly re-runs the structural analyses (ABC, the matrix). This module supplies the WHAT; the final module's calendar fixes the WHEN and WHO.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Turns & coverage: is the capital working?", "14", "<p>The first vital: how fast stock converts back to cash. Two lenses, one question.</p><p><b>Stock turnover.</b> The ratio: <b>COGS over average stock value</b> for a period (units work too — units sold over average units held — for item-level reads where value distorts). Turns of 12 means the stock pool converts to sales monthly; turns of 2 means the average naira sits six months. The mechanics matter less than the reading discipline: turns are computed PER ITEM and PER WAREHOUSE and then rolled — because the firm-wide number is an average of extremes that hides both diseases (the fast seller masking the dead pallet; Chapter 9 convicts the firm-wide-only read), and the interesting information is always the DISTRIBUTION: which items turn 20, which turn 1, and what each deserves.</p><p><b>Days of cover.</b> The operational twin: current stock over average daily sell-through = <b>how many days the shelf survives at current velocity</b>. Cover is the number planning conversations actually use (the reorder fields of Buying 2 ARE cover targets in disguise: reorder level = lead-time cover plus safety cover), and its per-item-per-warehouse read is the placement dial: Akure holding 9 days of a line while Ibadan holds 60 is the lateral conversation (module 6) wearing arithmetic.</p><p><b>Interpreting both directions.</b> The reading error to train out of clients: HIGH turns are not simply good, LOW not simply bad — each end has a failure mode. Turns too high / cover too thin: the firm is surfing stockout risk — every supply hiccup becomes a lost sale, expediting costs climb, and the apparent efficiency is fragility (the availability read, Chapter 5, is this number's conscience). Turns too low / cover too deep: capital parked — holding cost, expiry exposure (for dated goods, cover beyond remaining shelf life is a write-off on a schedule), and the opportunity cost the capital framing prices. The TARGET is a band, set per category (fast FMCG lines run high turns by nature; slow specialty lines run low legitimately), and the read is exceptions against the band — the item outside its band in either direction is the worklist.</p><p><b>Category benchmarks and the honest comparison.</b> Turns compare only within like: the pharmacy's fast analgesics against ITS fast analgesics over time, branch against branch on the SAME line, the firm against its trade's rough norms where known — never the antibiotic against the generator against the hair cream in one league table. The trend beats the level (the line whose turns halved in two quarters is a story regardless of where it sits), and the decomposition beats both: a falling turn is EITHER slowing sales or growing stock, and the two have different owners — the read that says WHICH (sell-through trend against stock trend, side by side) is the read that assigns the conversation correctly.</p><p><b>What turns feed.</b> Cover targets tune the planning fields (the quarterly exception review of Buying 2, now with its data source named); the turns distribution feeds ABC and the matrix (Chapter 4); thin-cover flags feed availability watch (Chapter 5); deep-cover flags feed the aging read (Chapter 3) before the aging read has to find them the hard way. One ratio, four consumers — which is why it leads the panel.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Aging & the dead-stock ladder", "14", "<p>The second vital: is the stock MOVING? Aging finds the capital that stopped, and the ladder is the discipline that releases it.</p><p><b>The aging read.</b> Stock stratified by <b>time since last movement</b> (last sale or issue — receipts don't count as movement for this purpose; a pallet that only ever arrives is exactly the patient we're looking for): 0-30 days, 31-60, 61-90, 91-180, 180+ — by item, by warehouse, VALUED. The value column is the read's teeth: <i>₦6.8m has not moved in 90+ days</i> lands differently than a list of SKUs, and per-warehouse stratification catches the network pattern (moving fine at Central, dead at the branch that over-requested it — a rebalance, not a markdown).</p><p><b>Dead stock, defined and owned.</b> The firm writes its definition — no movement in N days (90 for fast trade, 180 for slower; per category where honesty requires) — and the definition creates the WORKLIST: every line past the threshold has an owner and a next action, reviewed monthly. Without the written threshold, dead stock is a vibe discovered annually; with it, a pipeline worked continuously — the module 4 shrinkage logic applied to slowness.</p><p><b>The ladder — every rung cheaper than the next.</b> Module 3's markdown ladder generalised beyond expiry, ordered by value recovered:</p><ul><li><b>Push</b> — visibility first: front-of-shelf, staff spiffs, the counter suggestion; costs nothing, works more often than pride expects, and is the honest first test of whether the item is dead or merely hidden.</li><li><b>Rebalance</b> — the placement fix: dead HERE may be alive THERE — the lateral (module 6's economics: the double-move priced against the alternative rungs, and usually winning); the network's whole point is that a room's dead stock gets the other rooms' demand for free.</li><li><b>Markdown</b> — price against the calendar: the pre-approved discount ladder (the pricing-rule machinery, not per-carton negotiation), stepped down on schedule; for dated goods this IS module 3's ladder, deadline-driven.</li><li><b>Bundle &amp; convert</b> — the slow-mover riding the fast one (the bundle machinery from the selling track), or repack into the form that sells (Inv 2 — the bulk that died as cartons living as strips).</li><li><b>Liquidate</b> — the bulk exit at honest salvage: the institutional buyer, the trade channel, the clearance event; margin surrendered, capital and shelf recovered.</li><li><b>Write off</b> — the governed end (Inv 2's signatures) when the recovery costs more than the recovery; the rung nobody enjoys and delay makes MORE expensive: dated goods rot toward it, storage bleeds toward it, and the ladder's whole arithmetic is that month-one action at ANY rung beats month-twelve write-off at the last one.</li></ul><p><b>The capital-release framing.</b> The ladder's report line is not <i>losses taken</i> but <b>capital released</b>: ₦4.9m of dead value converted back to cash-and-shelf (at whatever recovery each rung achieved) is ₦4.9m re-deployable into stock that turns — and shelf space, in retail, is itself inventory capacity the dead stock was renting for free. Owners who resist markdowns as <i>losses</i> usually accept them as <i>release</i>; the framing is the consultant's tool, and the ladder-progress line on the monthly page (Chapter 7) is where it lives.</p><p><b>Upstream, always.</b> Dead stock is a message with a return address: the buy that misjudged (quantity or item — the planning conversation), the assortment that drifted from the customer, the branch allocation that shipped hope. The quarterly dead-stock cause read — what died and WHY — is the third of this track's letters to procurement, and the firms whose dead-stock rate falls are the ones that answered the mail.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — ABC & the value-velocity matrix", "13", "<p>The third structural read: not every item deserves equal attention, and this chapter is the formal machinery for aiming attention where money and movement actually are.</p><p><b>ABC, mechanically.</b> Items ranked by a period's value contribution (consumption value — quantity moved × cost — is the standard basis; sales value works for commercial lenses); the cumulative curve drawn; the classes cut: <b>A</b> — the vital few (typically ~20% of items carrying ~70-80% of value), <b>B</b> — the middle (~30% of items, ~15-25%), <b>C</b> — the trivial many (~50% of items, ~5-10%). The percentages are conventions, not laws; the SHAPE is the law — every real inventory is this skewed, and the skew is the entire management insight: treat all items equally and you spend 80% of attention on 10% of the money.</p><p><b>What ABC feeds — the consumers you already know.</b> The counting programme (module 4: A monthly, B quarterly, C annually — this chapter is where those classes come from); buying attention (A items get the sourcing rigour, the market tests, the drift eye at full alertness; C items get automation and sensible defaults — the Procurement 4 machinery pointed by class); service levels (A stockouts are emergencies, C stockouts are Tuesday — availability targets set per class, Chapter 5); and master-data care (A items' planning fields reviewed quarterly by name; C items' by exception). One analysis, four consumers — recomputed on a calendar (quarterly or semi-annual: assortments drift, and the ABC computed once in 2019 is Chapter 9's museum piece), with class CHANGES read as news: the C item climbing to B is a trend the buying should hear; the A item sliding is Chapter 3's early warning.</p><p><b>The value-velocity matrix — ABC's second dimension.</b> Value alone misleads: the high-value slow-mover and the high-value fast-mover are both <i>A</i> and need opposite handling. Cross value with VELOCITY (turns, Chapter 2) and read four quadrants:</p><ul><li><b>High value, fast</b> — the franchise: guard it (availability first, counting tightest, supplier redundancy per Procurement 1's concentration logic, the drift eye sharpest). Stockouts here are the expensive kind.</li><li><b>High value, slow</b> — the danger quadrant: big money sitting — deliberate stockholding only (the genuine specialty line held for strategic reasons, documented) or Chapter 3's patient in the making; reviewed by NAME monthly, cover capped, buying gated.</li><li><b>Low value, fast</b> — the automation quadrant: the workhorse tail that runs itself — auto-replenishment tuned and trusted, counting light, attention minimal by DESIGN (the win here is spending nothing managing it).</li><li><b>Low value, slow</b> — the pruning quadrant: the assortment's barnacles — the ladder's liquidate-and-delist candidates, the range review's kill list; every SKU here costs master-data care, shelf, and count minutes for near-zero contribution, and the periodic prune is inventory hygiene at the ASSORTMENT level.</li></ul><p><b>The matrix as the buying conversation.</b> One page, four quadrants, every item placed: this is the single most persuasive artefact the warehouse hands the buyer and the owner — it converts <i>we hold too much stock</i> (an argument) into <i>we hold ₦11m in the danger quadrant and here are the twelve items</i> (a worklist). The consultant who builds this page at every engagement builds it because it works.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Stockouts & availability: the other failure", "13", "<p>Every vital so far watched stock that EXISTS; this chapter watches the stock that DOESN'T — the empty shelf, the invisible failure, and the discipline that makes it visible and priced.</p><p><b>Why stockouts hide.</b> Overstock announces itself (the pallet in the aisle, the aging read, the value on the page); the stockout leaves NO RECORD by default — the customer asked, the shelf was empty, the sale never happened, and no ledger line marks the naira that walked out. A firm that reads only its stock reports therefore systematically over-corrects toward leanness (every visible number rewards holding less; the invisible number punishing it is never seen) — and the availability discipline exists to put the missing number on the page.</p><p><b>The two measurements.</b> <b>Zero-balance tracking</b> — the systemic read: days at zero (or below safety floor) per item per warehouse, from the bin history — cheap, automatic, and honest about DURATION (the line that zeroed for three hours vs three weeks); expressed as the <b>availability rate</b> — the percentage of item-days in stock, per branch, per class (the A-class availability target is the one that matters: 98%+ for the franchise quadrant is a common posture; C-class tolerates more). <b>Lost-sale capture</b> — the demand-side read: the counter and POS discipline of recording the ask that couldn't be served (the two-tap <i>not available</i> capture where the till supports it; the counter book where it doesn't) — imperfect by nature, invaluable in pattern: the item asked-for weekly that the assortment never carried is a listing decision the zero-balance read can never surface, because you cannot zero-balance what you never stocked.</p><p><b>Pricing the stockout.</b> The lost sale's cost is the margin forgone — plus the substitution risk (the customer who found it next door and liked next door), which for retail is the expensive part. The framing arithmetic for owners: an A-item stocking out for a week costs its weekly margin CERTAINLY and a customer relationship PROBABLY, and against that price the marginal cost of two more days' cover is usually trivial — which is the availability read disciplining the leanness instinct exactly as the aging read disciplines the hoarding one. The two reads together ARE the placement dial (module 6's language): stockouts say too little or wrong room; aging says too much or wrong room; and the dial is turned per item-warehouse, not per slogan.</p><p><b>The response taxonomy.</b> A stockout's cause assigns its fix: the trigger that never fired (planning fields — retune), the trigger that fired into a phantom pipeline (the stale PO — Procurement 5's sweep), the allocation that shorted the branch (module 6's policy and its relief), the run that slipped (the calendar's on-time read), the demand spike nobody could have held (logged, forgiven, and fed to the forecast), or the supplier failure (the scorecard's evidence). The monthly stockout review — incidents by cause, per branch — is short, and it is the read that turns availability from a percentage into a programme.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The reports shelf: which report answers which question", "13", "<p>The track has named a dozen reads; this chapter is the shelf — organised, as the earlier tracks organised theirs, by the QUESTION each report answers, because a consultant who maps question→report in one breath teaches clients to self-serve.</p><ul><li><b>What do we hold, right now?</b> — the stock balance: item × warehouse, quantity and value, the summary snapshot. The first report every client learns, and the one they over-use for questions it cannot answer — it shows the NOW, not the HOW.</li><li><b>How did it get this way?</b> — the stock ledger: the history, entry by entry, document by document (module 1's diagnostic superpower, formalised as the second report every consultant opens). Balance surprises decompose here; the pair together (balance for the what, ledger for the why) answer most support tickets before they escalate.</li><li><b>What will we hold?</b> — the projected view: the four numbers forward (actual − reserved + incoming), per item-warehouse — the planning read, and the one whose honesty depends on the ghost and phantom sweeps (the corrupted promise corrupts THIS page first).</li><li><b>Is it fresh?</b> — the batch and expiry reads (module 3's horizon and batch-wise balances): per-warehouse, worklist-formatted, decision-owned.</li><li><b>Is it true?</b> — module 4's accuracy rate and variance trends; module 5's stock-to-accounts bridge — the certificates, read as vitals.</li><li><b>Is it working?</b> — this module's panel: turns and cover (Ch. 2), aging and dead stock (Ch. 3), ABC and the matrix (Ch. 4), availability (Ch. 5).</li><li><b>Is the network placed right?</b> — module 6's dashboard: transit health, allocation log, lateral rate, branch scorecards.</li></ul><p><b>The shelf's disciplines.</b> Three, all restatements the certification has earned: <b>question first</b> — the report request that cannot name its question is answered with the question, not a report (the custom-report ticket that begins <i>can you add a column</i> usually dissolves when the underlying question meets the existing shelf); <b>cadence and owner</b> — every shelf report carries its rhythm and its name or it decays to decoration (Chapter 1's law; the final module's calendar enforces it); and <b>drill-down over export</b> — the reading culture to build is one where numbers are interrogated in the system (page → line → document — the provenance always three clicks away) rather than exported to spreadsheets where they detach from their evidence and begin their second life as fiction. The firm whose managers drill down argues from documents; the firm whose managers export argues from tabs — and only one of those arguments ends.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The monthly stock health page", "13", "<p>The panel assembles. One page, monthly, owned — the stock system's report card, and the conversation it scripts with the owner.</p><p><b>The page's lines — every one a chapter of this track:</b> <b>Stock value and trend</b> (total and by room family — the capital headline, module 1's MD page matured); <b>turns / cover</b> (firm and per-branch, against the category bands — Ch. 2); <b>aging profile</b> (the strata, valued, with the 90+ line bolded — Ch. 3); <b>dead stock value and ladder progress</b> (the pipeline: entered, actioned per rung, released — Ch. 3's capital-release line); <b>shrinkage rate</b> (module 4's number, trend and distribution flags); <b>expiry and markdown losses</b> (module 3's horizon exhaust, by cause); <b>availability rate</b> (per class, with A-class bolded, and the month's stockout causes — Ch. 5); <b>accuracy rate</b> (module 4's certificate); and <b>the exceptions line</b> (open investigations, unclosed runs, negative flags — the aging exceptions from every module's machinery, counted). Twelve numbers, one page; every number drills to documents.</p><p><b>Targets and bands.</b> Each line carries its target or band, set at implementation and reviewed annually: shrinkage under X%, A-availability over Y%, dead stock under Z% of holding, accuracy over W% — the numbers themselves are the client's to choose (with the consultant supplying trade norms and the arithmetic of each choice), but the CHOOSING is mandatory: a page without targets is a weather report; a page with them is a management instrument, because every line now reads green, amber, or a conversation.</p><p><b>The MD conversation, scripted.</b> The page's monthly reading with the owner follows the same shape every time — which is its virtue: headline (the capital and its trend), the ambers (each with its owner's one-paragraph story and next action — the page holds people to narratives, not blame), the releases and saves (the ladder's line, the markdown that pre-empted a write-off, the availability point recovered — the wins made visible, because a page that only carries problems teaches people to dread it), and the ONE decision the month actually needs from the owner (the danger-quadrant review, the liquidation approval, the target reset). Twenty minutes; the owner leaves knowing their largest asset's condition; and the reading's regularity is what makes December boring — module 4's certificate, generalised to the whole system.</p><p><b>The page's owner.</b> One name — the stock controller, the ops lead, the founder-until-delegated — who compiles (fifteen minutes, because every line is standing machinery), reads first, chases the ambers before the meeting, and owns the trends between meetings. The page without an owner joins Chapter 9's bestiary; the page WITH one is, for the stock system, what the run calendar was for the network: the heartbeat that makes everything else rhythmic. The final module hands this page its calendar slot and its place in the whole track's operating structure.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's health year", "16", "<p>The reading discipline, lived. Four quarters from first page to managed system — every chapter clocking in.</p><p><b>The first page (Ch. 1, 3, 7).</b> Month one's compile is the shock the framing predicted: total stock ₦71m across the network... and the aging read's 90+ line holds <b>₦6.8m — 9.6% of the pool — that has not moved in a quarter</b>, concentrated in three stories the value column tells at a glance: an over-bought vitamin range (the promo that under-performed), a branch allocation that shipped hope to Abeokuta, and a tail of 140 C-class SKUs dying quietly. The MD's first reaction is the classic — <i>write it off?</i> — and the consultant's reframe is the chapter's: this is capital in prison, and the ladder is the release programme, cheapest rungs first.</p><p><b>The ladder's two quarters (Ch. 3).</b> Worked monthly, owned by name: PUSH recovers ₦900k at full margin (the hidden, not dead — front-shelf and counter suggestion did it); REBALANCE moves ₦1.1m of the Abeokuta allocation to where it sells (two laterals, priced and approved — module 6's machinery); MARKDOWN steps ₦2.3m through the pre-approved ladder (average recovery 71% of full price — against the write-off's zero); BUNDLE moves ₦400k of slow syrup riding the analgesic franchise; LIQUIDATE clears ₦200k of the C-tail to an institutional buyer at salvage. Two quarters in: <b>₦4.9m released</b>, the 90+ line down to 2.8%, and the write-off rung needed for just ₦180k of genuinely dead stock — signed, coded, and fed upstream: the dead-stock cause read hands procurement two findings (the vitamin buy's quantity logic; the Abeokuta allocation's velocity assumption), and both planning fields are retuned.</p><p><b>The matrix repricing attention (Ch. 4).</b> The quarter-two ABC refresh and the value-velocity matrix land on one page: 58 A items; the danger quadrant holds ₦9.4m across eleven items — nine of them deliberate (the specialty lines, documented as strategic holds with capped cover) and TWO of them drift (reviewed by name, cover halved, buying gated). The automation quadrant — 210 fast cheap SKUs — gets its planning fields tuned once and its counting lightened; the pruning quadrant's review delists 60 SKUs at the annual range review. Buying attention now follows the matrix, and the drift eye sharpens exactly where Chapter 4 pointed it.</p><p><b>Availability, lifted by placement (Ch. 5).</b> The first availability read: A-class at 94.2% network-wide — but the DISTRIBUTION says the story: Central 98%, Ibadan 96%, Akure 89% — the new branch's fields still finding their level (module 6's day-90 retune, caught mid-flight). The stockout cause review runs monthly: two phantom-pipeline incidents (stale POs swept — Procurement 5's ritual now triggered from THIS read), one allocation short (policy-governed, relief-dated), the rest field-tuning. By quarter four: <b>A-class at 98.1%</b> — lifted not by holding more stock (total holding actually FELL ₦3.2m over the year as the ladder released and the fields tuned) but by placing it right: the placement dial, turned with both hands.</p><p><b>The year's page (Ch. 7).</b> December's read: value ₦67.8m (down, deliberately — the capital released funded two months of the Akure expansion's stock); turns up 22% network-wide; dead stock 2.1% and pipelined; shrinkage 0.41% (module 4's steady state); A-availability 98.1%; accuracy 97.3%; every amber owned, every line three clicks from its documents. Twenty minutes with the MD, one decision (the next range review's date), and the sentence that closes the case study and sells the module: <i>the reports didn't improve the stock — the reading did.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the seventh law", "13", "<p>The reading layer's scar tissue. Symptom → disease → fix.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the firm-wide-only read.</b> Symptom: healthy averages, dying branches; turns of 8 masking the item at 20 and the pallet at 0. Disease: rollup reading (Ch. 2) — the average of extremes hiding both diseases. Fix: distribution reads by construction — per item, per warehouse, exceptions against bands — the rollup reserved for the headline it honestly is.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — dead stock as December's surprise.</b> Symptom: the annual stocktake's write-off shock; the aisle pallet everyone stopped seeing. Disease: no threshold, no worklist, no ladder (Ch. 3) — slowness invisible until it is only a write-off. Fix: the written definition, the monthly aging read with owners, the ladder worked cheapest-rung-first, and the release framing that gets owners moving.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the invisible stockout.</b> Symptom: lean, efficient... and quietly shrinking sales; customers drifting next door. Disease: the unrecorded failure (Ch. 5) — every visible number rewarding leanness, the punishing number never captured. Fix: zero-balance tracking and availability rates on the page, lost-sale capture at the counter, and the stockout cause review assigning fixes monthly.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the museum ABC.</b> Symptom: counting and buying attention aimed by an analysis computed years ago; A-items of 2019 guarding the assortment of today. Disease: the structural reads never recomputed (Ch. 4). Fix: the quarterly refresh on the calendar, class changes read as news, the matrix rebuilt beside it.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the exported argument.</b> Symptom: every management meeting runs on spreadsheets three exports old; numbers no one can trace; arguments that never end. Disease: the drill-down culture never built (Ch. 6) — figures detached from their documents living second lives as fiction. Fix: the reads interrogated in the system, provenance three clicks away, and the meeting discipline that any disputed number is opened live, not debated from tabs.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the ownerless page.</b> Symptom: the health page compiled for three months, then quietly never again; targets unset; ambers unowned. Disease: the panel without its heartbeat (Ch. 7) — reading treated as a report, not a rhythm. Fix: one name, a calendar slot, targets chosen, and the twenty-minute MD reading that makes the compile matter — the final module's structure holds the slot.</p><p><b>The seventh law.</b> The tree is the map; movements are documents; identity is operational; the count is a dialogue; valuation is written at the moment of movement; the network is one stock in many rooms. This module: <b>stock health is read, not felt — the numbers exist the day the disciplines exist, and capital works exactly as hard as the rhythm that reads it.</b> Turns ask if it's working, aging asks if it's moving, the matrix aims the attention, availability prices the empty shelf, and the page holds the panel to a heartbeat. One module remains: the structure that holds every rhythm, role, and rule this track has built — and hands the whole system to someone who isn't you.</p>"),
]

